1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Where may a card number live in your system?

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:07,128
Picture a shop that keeps card numbers in its orders table, just in case it needs them for refunds.

3
00:00:07,178 --> 00:00:09,000
Then the numbers spread.

4
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:11,544
A report reads them every morning.

5
00:00:11,594 --> 00:00:13,848
A backup copies them every night.

6
00:00:13,898 --> 00:00:16,560
And every developer's laptop can reach them.

7
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:24,072
Now each of those systems holds card numbers, so each of them must be checked: its network, its logins, its logs and its changes.

8
00:00:24,122 --> 00:00:27,936
One weak laptop can leak every card the shop has ever seen.

9
00:00:27,986 --> 00:00:29,904
Those checks come from one rulebook.

10
00:00:29,954 --> 00:00:37,632
PCI DSS is the card industry's security rulebook, and it checks every system that touches a card number, or can reach one.

11
00:00:37,682 --> 00:00:40,584
So the way out is to have less to check.

12
00:00:40,634 --> 00:00:42,456
Think of a coat check.

13
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:46,296
Coats go into one locked room, and everyone else gets a ticket.

14
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:50,472
The ticket brings your coat back, but it is worth nothing to a thief.

15
00:00:50,522 --> 00:00:58,800
Card numbers work the same way: they go into one locked room, and every other system gets a ticket, the card token, never the number.

16
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:02,136
In this system, the locked room is the card zone.

17
00:01:02,186 --> 00:01:07,488
It has its own database, its own keys and its own logins, and it shares none of them.

18
00:01:07,538 --> 00:01:13,344
It has only two doors: one for buyers' browsers, and one for partner systems like ecom.

19
00:01:13,394 --> 00:01:17,616
The card goes straight through the first door into the room, never through ecom.

20
00:01:17,666 --> 00:01:21,624
ecom only ever holds tickets, so a leak there leaks no card.

21
00:01:21,674 --> 00:01:27,840
ecom still gets a check, just a smaller one: it shows the card form, and it takes payments with tickets.

22
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:29,712
Smaller, not none.

23
00:01:29,762 --> 00:01:36,816
In a bank, the card zone runs inside the bank's own secured network, with the bank's guards and firewalls around it.

24
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:40,128
We deliver the locked room; the bank owns the building.

25
00:01:40,178 --> 00:01:43,680
Next: how ecom and the card zone talk to each other.
